1: # K39 working: the v=0 marked source, Cartier line and completed-family transport
2: 
3: The source coordinates are those of SM.7:
4: \[
5:  Y^2=X^3+(1-2v)X^2+TX+v^2,\qquad G=(0,v),\qquad
6:  T_0=v^2-2v,\qquad q=T-T_0.
7: \]
8: This note retains the original normalized E7 source
9: \(y^2=x^3+3rx^2+tx+1\), its actual line and its chord-normalized
10: linearization. No completed-family return is assigned yet.
11: 
12: ## 1. Literal simultaneous resolution selected by G
13: 
14: Set
15: \[
16:  A=Y-v,\quad B=Y+v,\quad C=X,\quad
17:  D=T+X^2+(1-2v)X.
18: \]
19: The equation is exactly \(AB=CD\), and the inverse is
20: \[
21:  X=C,\quad Y=(A+B)/2,\quad v=(B-A)/2,\quad
22:  T=D-C^2-(1-B+A)C.
23: \]
24: The two small resolutions are the two rank-one matrix factorizations.
25: The one retaining the marked section is
26: \[
27:  A=Dz,\qquad C=Bz.
28: \]
29: On its displayed affine chart,
30: \[
31:  v=(B-Dz)/2,\quad X=Bz,\quad Y=(Dz+B)/2,\quad
32:  T=D-B^2z^2-(1-B+Dz)Bz.
33: \]
34: The complementary chart is \(D=Aw,\ B=Cw\), with \(w=1/z\);
35: its parameter is \(v=(Cw-A)/2\). Each chart is smooth, and projection
36: to v is a submersion because its derivative in B, respectively A, is
37: \(1/2\), respectively \(-1/2\). The actual section is
38: \[
39:  G:\quad z=0,\quad B=2v,\quad D=T.
40: \]
41: It extends across the central node. In the other small resolution
42: \(A=Cz,\ D=Bz\), the same section has \(z=T/(2v)\); its closure
43: is the blowup of the base ideal \((T,v)\), not a section at the
44: origin. This is the actual marked morphism choosing the first resolution.
45: 
46: At \(v=0\), \(B=Dz\) and
47: \[
48:  T=D(1-z^2-Dz^4).
49: \]
50: Thus the central I2 consists of the exceptional component
51: \(C_1:D=0\) and the proper component
52: \(C_0:1-z^2-Dz^4=0\). The marked section meets C1 at z=0;
53: the zero section meets C0. Their intersections are z=+1 and z=-1.
54: The selected critical curve is
55: \[
56:  z=1,\qquad B=v,\qquad D=-v,
57: \]
58: which maps to \(X=v,Y=0,T=T_0\). Hence it extends to the z=+1
59: intersection. The other node of the central I2 is the z=-1 intersection.
60: 
61: ## 2. The actual 2G Cartier specialization
62: 
63: The exact doubling formula is
64: \[
65:  X(2G)=\frac{T^2}{4v^2}-(1-2v),\qquad
66:  Y(2G)=-v-\frac{T}{2v}X(2G).
67: \]
68: Consequently its lift has \(z=-2v/T\). In the small-resolution chart,
69: the equation \(zT+2v=0\) factors as
70: \[
71:  zT+2v
72:    =B\bigl(1-Bz^3-(1-2v)z^2\bigr).
73: \]
74: The B=0 factor is the extraneous -G section, introduced when the
75: formula \(z=X/(Y+v)\) is cleared of denominators. The actual 2G
76: closure has the Cartier equation
77: \[
78:                   1-Bz^3-(1-2v)z^2=0.
79: \]
80: It restricts at v=0 to \(1-z^2-Dz^4=0\), with multiplicity one,
81: and it does not contain C1. It misses G because at z=0 its defining
82: function is 1. Away from T=0, the displayed doubling formula tends
83: to O. Therefore the relative divisor \(2G-O\) specializes to C0
84: on this local source.
85: 
86: This also gives the global specialization on the compact minimal
87: source. The III* component degrees of \(2G-O\) are zero on every
88: nearby source and remain zero on its unchanged simultaneous root
89: charts. Any extra vertical divisor there is an integral whole
90: fibre, by the component intersection kernel. At the other finite
91: fibres the section tends to O and no component is added. Finally
92: \((2G-O)\cdot O=1=C_0\cdot O\), so the possible additional whole
93: fibre coefficient is zero. Thus the actual line for (a,b)=(2,1)
94: has limit \(O(C_0)\). Its class is retained even though the generic
95: Picard point 2G tends to zero.
96: 
97: For (a,b)=(1,2), G itself extends as the marked section above, so
98: the actual central line is \(O(G_0-O)\); it has component degrees
99: (-1,+1) on (C0,C1), and its generic point is nonzero two-torsion.
100: 
101: ## 3. Exact global Hom sections and their source-parameter winding
102: 
103: In the original normalized coordinates, put
104: \[
105:  f_{21}=\frac{y+1+(t/2)x}{x},\qquad
106:  f_{12}=\frac{y-1-(t/2)x}{x}.
107: \]
108: For (a,b)=(2,1), the numerator is the tangent line at -G;
109: for (a,b)=(1,2), it is the tangent line at +G. The denominator
110: is the vertical chord through G and -G. Their horizontal divisors
111: are exactly
112: \[
113:  D_{21}=(2G)-O-G+(-G),\qquad
114:  D_{12}=G-O-(-G)+(-2G).
115: \]
116: These are the actual lines of target-point linearizations for the
117: forward suspension by -bG. At III* the component contributions
118: cancel, because G and -G have the same nonidentity component and
119: 2G,-2G,O have the identity component. At finite fibres the displayed
120: functions have no whole-fibre zero or pole. Their vertical divisor
121: is therefore a multiple of the infinity fibre. Intersecting with O,
122: using the explicit polynomial coordinates of 2G, gives
123: \[
124:                 \operatorname{div}(f_{ab})=D_{ab}-F_\infty.
125: \]
126: Hence in the canonical meromorphic divisor frame the actual
127: linearization with selected simple whole zero is
128: \[
129:                        \phi_{ab}=\frac{t-t_0}{f_{ab}}.
130: \]
131: This fixes a concrete normalization of its scalar parameter.
132: 
133: Under \(X=u^2x,Y=u^3y,T=u^4t,v=u^3\), define
134: \[
135:  F_{21}=\frac{Y+v+(T/(2v))X}{X},\qquad
136:  F_{12}=\frac{Y-v-(T/(2v))X}{X}.
137: \]
138: Literal substitution gives \(f_{ab}=u^{-1}F_{ab}\). Consequently
139: \[
140:                       \phi_{ab}=\frac{q}{vF_{ab}}.              \tag{SZ.1}
141: \]
142: This is invariant under the marked mu3 coordinate quotient because v
143: is invariant. At the selected node \(X=v,Y=0,T=T_0\),
144: \[
145:  F_{21}=v/2,\qquad F_{12}=-v/2.
146: \]
147: In the local canonical line frame the Hom factor divided by q is
148: therefore exactly \(+2/v^2\), respectively \(-2/v^2\). It winds
149: twice negatively on a positive source-v loop.
150: 
151: The actual selected nodal Tate point of G is SM.7's
152: \[
153:  P_G=\frac{1+\sqrt{1+v}}{1-\sqrt{1+v}},\qquad vP_G\longrightarrow-4.
154: \]
155: The Tate base factor satisfies
156: \[
157:  q_T/q=-\frac{v}{64(1+v)^3}+O(q).
158: \]
159: Thus the three recorded source-parameter windings are respectively
160: qT/q:+1, PG:-1, and Hom/q:-2. In a sufficiently small selected collar,
161: the theta ratio identifying the actual Cartier line has a nonzero
162: limit 1 at the chosen nodal branch, so it does not change the latter
163: Hom-unit winding. This last branch comparison must also be retained
164: on the other node branch when the completion is glued.
165: 
166: ## 4. Exact logarithmic winding matrix and the remaining completion map
167: 
168: Order the local torus coordinates by (source Tate, line Tate), and the
169: two q-periods by (source, suspension). The linear part of the positive
170: source-loop unit winding is
171: \[
172:  W_{ab}=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&-2\end{pmatrix}
173:        =\binom1{-a}(1\ \ b),\qquad ab=2.
174: \]
175: It has rank one. The literal integral basis
176: \[
177:  P=\begin{pmatrix}1&-b\\-a&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad\det P=-1
178: \]
179: diagonalizes it: \(P^{-1}W_{ab}P=\operatorname{diag}(-1,0)\).
180: The physical delta cocharacter becomes \(P^{-1}(0,1)^t=(-b,-1)^t\);
181: it must not be replaced by a new unit vertical character.
182: 
183: This is an exact statement about the recorded logarithmic winding,
184: not yet an isomorphism to a globally split product of Tate curves.
185: The required remaining map is the relative completion over the
186: source-v loop, with all bounded q/v units, the selected-node Cartier
187: frame, the physical delta character and every finite-cap norm retained.
188: The tentative diagonal parameters q/v and q must be justified by that
189: actual map before their degeneration can be used to compute the return.
190: The source resolution alone does not supply it: the generic suspension
191: translations acquire nontrivial behaviour on the central components,
192: and their source-Picard limits do not identify the relative line maps.
193: 
194: The exact algebraic identities in Sections1--3, and the winding-matrix
195: factorization, pass in code/part3_fourfold/source_zero_resolution.py,
196: with its JSON and captured outputs. No value of the completed-family
197: source-loop return is encoded in that certificate.
198: 
199: ## 5. Normalization blowup and the retained Cartier line
200: 
201: The normalization of the selected nodal fibre has the exact map
202: \[
203:  X=s^2-1,\qquad Y=s(s^2-1-v),\qquad T=v^2-2v.
204: \]
205: Its lift into the marked small resolution is
206: \[
207:                     z=\frac{s+1}{s^2+s-v}.                    \tag{SZ.2}
208: \]
209: The unique indeterminacy is \((s,v)=(-1,0)\). Blow up this point of
210: \(P^1_s\times D_v\). On the chart \(s=r-1,\ v=rt\), the lift is
211: \[
212:                          z=(r-1-t)^{-1}.                     \tag{SZ.3}
213: \]
214: Thus the exceptional divisor \(E:r=0\) maps to \(C_1\), while the strict
215: central fibre \(t=0\) maps to \(C_0\). The complementary blowup chart
216: extends the marked point \(G:s=-1\) to \(z=0\) on \(E\). This is the
217: literal source normalization lift; no choice of an abstract blowup
218: identification is used.
219: 
220: Put \(c=\sqrt{1+v}\), using the single-valued unit branch with \(c(0)=1\),
221: and let \(D_\pm\) be the strict transforms of \(s=\pm c\). Only \(D_-\)
222: passes through the centre. The exceptional valuation of \(s+c\) is one
223: and that of \(s-c\) is zero. Consequently
224: \[
225:  \operatorname{div}\frac{s-c}{s+c}=D_+-D_--E,\qquad
226:                          O(D_+-D_-)=O(E).                   \tag{SZ.4}
227: \]
228: For example, on the displayed chart
229: \(s+c=r(1+t/2)+O(r^2)\), whereas \(s-c=-2+O(r)\).
230: At the intersection of \(D_-\) with \(E\), \(t=-2\), and (SZ.3)
231: gives the selected node \(z=1\).
232: 
233: The actual source line restricts to
234: \[
235:                            M_S=O(-aE),\qquad a=1,2.          \tag{SZ.5}
236: \]
237: For \(a=1\), the divisor of the marked section \(G\) is its strict
238: transform through the blowup centre, while \(O\) misses that centre;
239: their difference has class \(-E\). For \(a=2\), the generic normalized
240: point \(2G\) alone does not compute the Cartier restriction. Section 2
241: shows that its actual closure adds \(C_0\). On the blown-up
242: normalization \(C_0\) is the strict central fibre, with class \(-E\);
243: the strict marked-section part contributes the other \(-E\).
244: Equivalently the restriction has degree \(+2\) on \(E=C_1\), whereas
245: both sides have degree zero on every generic normalization fibre.
246: The Picard group of the blowup over the contractible disc is generated
247: by the generic-fibre degree and \(E\). These two degrees determine the
248: displayed line, including its sign.
249: 
250: There is also an exact local line map at the two branches. In the
251: unimodular Cox charts a change of source line frame \(V\mapsto gV\)
252: acts on the two complex normal coordinates by
253: \[
254:                        (c_0,c_1)\longmapsto(g^{-1}c_0,gc_1).
255: \]
256: After the prescribed conjugation of the negative-weight coordinate,
257: the unitary part of \(g\) multiplies both spinor coordinates. The
258: positive real part contracts through positive diagonal maps and does
259: not change that clutch. Thus the spinor bundle on this source
260: normalization model is literally
261: \[
262:  \bigl(O(D_+)\oplus O(-D_-)\bigr)\otimes M_S,\qquad
263:              c_1(\det)=(1-2a)E.                             \tag{SZ.6}
264: \]
265: Here the untwisted normal line is \(O(-D_+-D_-)\), obtained by taking
266: the cross product of the normalization derivatives in the
267: canonically trivialized ambient source determinant. The two branch
268: zeros are simple, as the transverse nodal equation directly shows.
269: Formula (SZ.6) records the actual Cartier-frame effect. It does not
270: assert that this local source model has already been embedded as the
271: fixed trace in a product orbit bordism.
272: 
273: The ordered branches in (SZ.6) are part of that formula's data.
274: Interchanging their roles gives instead
275: \[
276:  \bigl(O(D_-)\oplus O(-D_+)\bigr)\otimes M_S,\qquad
277:                          c_1(\det)=-(1+2a)E.                 \tag{SZ.6a}
278: \]
279: The single-valued choice of \(c=\sqrt{1+v}\) does not itself identify
280: either ordering with the physical gamma ordering in the completed
281: family. That identification remains part of the suspension comparison.
282: 
283: ## 6. The exact primitive suspension square
284: 
285: Let \(U\) denote the rational target-point lift of translation by
286: \(-G\) on \(O(G-O)\):
287: \[
288:                  (p,w)\longmapsto
289:                  \left(p-G,\frac{w}{X(p-G)}\right).          \tag{SZ.7}
290: \]
291: The divisor identity making this a line map is
292: \[
293:  (G)-O-t_G^*((G)-O)=(G)+(-G)-2O=\operatorname{div}X
294: \]
295: on each ordinary elliptic fibre. The displayed factor is evaluated
296: at the target point. This convention is the same convention as the
297: actual coefficient \(\phi=q/(vF_{12})\) in Section 3.
298: 
299: For a point \(r=(X,Y)\) on the source curve, the addition formula gives
300: \[
301:  X(r+G)=\left(\frac{Y-v}{X}\right)^2-(1-2v)-X,
302: \]
303: and the source equation then gives the exact identity
304: \[
305:                          X(r+G)X(r)=-2vF_{12}(r).            \tag{SZ.8}
306: \]
307: Therefore the suspension in the \((a,b)=(1,2)\) family is
308: \[
309:                     B_\lambda=-2\lambda q\,U^2.             \tag{SZ.9}
310: \]
311: This is an equality of actual rational line maps, not just of their
312: generic Picard points. It retains the scalar and the selected
313: whole-fibre factor.
314: 
315: The local nonzero line above the exceptional source component also
316: has a literal Cox presentation. Write
317: \[
318:  D=s_0t_0,\quad A=s_1t_0,\quad B=s_0t_1,\quad C=s_1t_1,
319: \]
320: where the auxiliary action has weights \((1,1,-1,-1)\). Since
321: \(O(G-O)|_{C_1}=O(1)\), the nonzero line coordinate has auxiliary
322: weight \(+1\). Fixing it to one leaves the actual coordinates
323: \[
324:   (s_0,s_1,t_0,t_1),\qquad (s_0,s_1)\ne(0,0),\qquad
325:                    v=\frac{s_0t_1-s_1t_0}{2}.               \tag{SZ.10}
326: \]
327: The Hessian determinant of this quadratic is \(1/16\), so this map
328: is explicitly nondegenerate. The physical delta action is retained:
329: it has weights \((-1,-1,+1,+1)\) after this gauge choice. For the
330: degree-two line the same gauge has the residual \(\mu_2\) action;
331: it must not be discarded.
332: 
333: Equations (SZ.7)--(SZ.10) identify the component exchange whose graph
334: must be resolved. They do not yet identify the quotient of its
335: relative completion with the original compact family: \(U\) has
336: component poles, and its chosen source model changes by a flop at
337: the central curve. A generic translation logarithm across \(v=0\)
338: has therefore not been used.
339: 
340: ## 7. Finite-root units and the remaining comparison
341: 
342: The source-parameter pole of the selected Hom coefficient is not a
343: pole of every cap coefficient. For the good III* root of the
344: \((a,b)=(1,2)\) case, put \(T=z^{-4},X=z^{-2}X',Y=z^{-3}Y'\).
345: Then literal substitution in (SZ.1) gives
346: \[
347:  \phi_{12}=
348:  \frac{1-(v^2-2v)z^4}
349:  {-1/2+vz^3Y'/X'-v^2z^6/X'}.
350:                                                                \tag{SZ.11}
351: \]
352: Its generic central-component value is \(-2\), with no winding in
353: \(v\). For a reduced III root \(m=4p-1\) in the \((a,b)=(2,1)\)
354: case, the analogous substitution gives
355: \[
356:  \phi_{21}=
357:  \frac{1-(v^2-2v)z^m}
358:  {1/2+vz^{m-p}Y'/X'+v^2z^{m+2p}/X'}.
359:                                                                \tag{SZ.12}
360: \]
361: The generic central-component value is \(+2\). The certificate checks
362: the good root and the basic reduced root literally. Extension through
363: all resolved root components still belongs to the required completed
364: family comparison; the generic values alone do not supply it.
365: 
366: The present exact remaining map is the relative graph completion of
367: the suspension (SZ.9), or its degree-two-line counterpart, through
368: the central flop, with the chosen finite-root line maps and physical
369: delta kept. Rescaling \(\lambda=v^2\lambda_0\) cancels the selected
370: Hom-unit pole, but changes the actual scalar loop by two turns.
371: The scalar-loop contribution and every cap gluing must consequently
372: be retained in any argument using that rescaling. No numerical
373: source-loop return follows merely from the signature of the blown-up
374: normalization or from (SZ.6).
375: 
376: All displayed algebraic identities in Sections 5--7 are checked in
377: code/part3_fourfold/source_zero_trace.py, with JSON and captured
378: outputs. Its status explicitly stops at the source, Cartier and
379: suspension identities.
380: 
381: ## 8. The local origin link, with its actual circle charge
382: 
383: The nonzero degree-\(a\) line gauge in (SZ.10) has the residual scalar
384: group \(\mu_a\). Its origin link is \(S^7/\mu_a\); after conjugating
385: the two negative delta weight coordinates, the orbit map is literally
386: the positive Hopf projection
387: \[
388:                   S^7/\mu_a\longrightarrow \mathbb{CP}^3.
389: \]
390: Its principal-circle Euler class is \(a h\): the original unit Hopf
391: transition function is raised to the \(a\)-th power by the quotient of
392: its fibre by \(\mu_a\). In particular the degree-two line has charge
393: two, not one. With \(h\) positive and \(\int h^3=1\), the oriented
394: characteristic value of this actual link is
395: \[
396:  \frac{4(ah)^3-p_1(T\mathbb{CP}^3)(ah)}{24}
397:                     =\frac{a(a^2-1)}6.                     \tag{SZ.13}
398: \]
399: Here \(p_1(T\mathbb{CP}^3)=4h^2\) follows directly from the Euler
400: sequence. These values are zero for \(a=1\), and one for \(a=2\).
401: 
402: For comparison, the sphere-bundle expression belonging to the ordered
403: local determinant in (SZ.6), on the blowup whose intersection form is
404: \(E^2=-1\), is \(-a(a-1)/2\). The reversed order (SZ.6a) instead gives
405: \(-a(a+1)/2\). These are computations on the specified local bundles.
406: No global equality between their sum and the actual source return is
407: asserted: the relative graph quotient must determine which ordered
408: trace occurs, how many origin-link boundary components occur, and
409: their boundary orientations.
410: 
411: The remaining geometry is now localized more precisely. Translation
412: by \(-G\) contracts the central component \(C_0\) in the marked total
413: source and replaces it by the other small-resolution curve. The
414: Cartier multiplier in (SZ.7) must be carried through that graph.
415: The successive source models for \(U^b\) form the actual component
416: flop chain; only its quotient by the suspension gives a candidate
417: compact orbit bordism. A construction using the two moment chambers
418: of (SZ.10) must exhibit the same overlap map as (SZ.7), retain the
419: bounded line units and the finite-cap characters, and identify the
420: physical gamma ordering. This is the precise uncompleted map.
421: 
422: ## 9. The actual Cartier overlap of the two source flop chambers
423: 
424: The chamber overlap in the preceding paragraph can be made literal.
425: Write \(p'=p-G\), and let \(F_2=1-Bz^3-(1-2v)z^2\) be the actual
426: Cartier equation from Section 2. The addition formula gives
427: \[
428:  X'=\frac{F_2}{z^2},\qquad Y'=v-\frac{X'}z.
429: \]
430: Set \(A'=Y'-v,\ B'=Y'+v,\ C'=X'\), and
431: \(D'=T+X'^2+(1-2v)X'\). Direct substitution gives
432: \[
433:                    A'=-C'/z,\qquad D'=-B'/z.                \tag{SZ.14}
434: \]
435: These are exactly the equations of the other small resolution of
436: \(A'B'=C'D'\). In particular the map contracts the proper component
437: \(C_0\), where \(F_2=0\), and the ratio of the other ruling is
438: \(-1/z\). The marked target small resolution uses the first ruling.
439: This identifies the graph of translation by \(-G\) with the actual
440: Atiyah flop in these coordinates.
441: 
442: Use its common Cox coordinates
443: \[
444:  A'=s_0t_0,\quad B'=s_1t_1,\quad C'=s_0t_1,\quad D'=s_1t_0,
445: \]
446: with auxiliary weights \(+1\) on the \(s_i\) and \(-1\) on the
447: \(t_i\). The target ruling has coordinate \(s_0/s_1\), and its marked
448: section \(G\) has \(s_0=0\). The domain ruling has coordinate
449: \(t_0/t_1=-1/z\), and its zero section \(O\) has \(t_1=0\).
450: Let \(V\) have auxiliary weight \(+1\). On the target chart the
451: canonical meromorphic coefficient of the line \(O(G-O)\) is
452: \[
453:                              w_{\rm target}=V/s_0;
454: \]
455: on the domain chart it is
456: \[
457:                              w_{\rm source}=Vt_1.
458: \]
459: For clarity, on \(s_1=1\) the regular line frame is the divisor
460: frame \(1/(s_0/s_1)\), whereas on \(t_0=1\) it is the divisor
461: frame \(t_1/t_0\). These two displayed coefficients are exactly
462: their expressions in the canonical meromorphic frame. Hence
463: \[
464:                  \frac{w_{\rm target}}{w_{\rm source}}
465:                        =\frac1{s_0t_1}=\frac1{X'},           \tag{SZ.15}
466: \]
467: which is the actual multiplier in (SZ.7). Thus the source line map
468: through the flop is the identity in the common Cox coordinate \(V\),
469: not merely an abstract equality of line degrees. Fixing the nonzero
470: \(V\) gives the two moment chambers of the same \(C^4\) model.
471: For the \(a\)-th tensor power it gives the same two chambers of
472: \(C^4/\mu_a\), retaining the physical delta action already recorded.
473: 
474: The actual degree-two line has a compatible explicit Cartier map
475: to the square of the degree-one line. Namely
476: \[
477:  r(p)=X(p-G)=\frac{2vF_{21}(p)}{X(p)}
478:               =\frac{F_2}{z^2}.                            \tag{SZ.16}
479: \]
480: Its divisor is the difference between the actual \(2G-O\) divisor
481: and twice \(G-O\), on this collision neighbourhood. Multiplication
482: by \(r\) is consequently the line isomorphism
483: \(O(2G-O)\longrightarrow O(G-O)^{\otimes2}\). The expression
484: \(F_2/z^2\) retains the central component and has no extra factor of
485: \(v\). At a target point \(p'=p-G\),
486: \[
487:  F_{21}(p')=\frac{r(p')r(p)}{2v}.
488: \]
489: Transporting the actual suspension through (SZ.16) therefore gives
490: \[
491:  \frac{r(p')}{r(p)}\frac{\lambda q}{vF_{21}(p')}
492:                          =\frac{2\lambda q}{X(p')^2}.       \tag{SZ.17}
493: \]
494: 
495: Let \(U_a\) denote the tensor-power version of (SZ.7), with target
496: multiplier \(X(p-G)^{-a}\). Both allowed source/line sectors now have
497: the exact common form
498: \[
499:            B_{a,b}=2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q\,U_a^b,
500:                        \qquad ab=2.                        \tag{SZ.18}
